    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Adaptation, a reduction of responsiveness upon repeated stimulation, is a universal finding across sensory systems. Within the visual system, adaptation has been studied in detail for responses to color, shape, and motion, but not to an equivalent extent for depth. The ability to perceive depth in the visual scene is a fundamental aspect of human vision, allowing us to see the world in three dimensions. A key cue contributing to depth is the capacity to process differences between images viewed by the left and right eyes, or binocular retinal disparity. While depth adaptation has been studied psychophysically, there has been limited exploration of the neural underpinnings of adaptation to prolonged exposure to binocular retinal disparity, specifically in the context of electrophysiological measurements as a direct measure of neural activity. This study aims to bridge this gap by examining the adaptation effects on retinal disparity sensitivity and the potential involvement of two distinct disparity sub-systems. Dynamic Random Dot Stereograms (DRDS) were used to isolate binocular disparity cues. Following prolonged viewing of DRDS with or without disparity, Steady-State Visual Evoked Potential (SSVEP) amplitudes were recorded to swept disparity testing conditions, with adaptation effects evaluated via comparison of response threshold and shape with or without prior disparity adaptation. Two types of disparity adaptors and tests were used. The first was a disparity plane stimulus that contained only absolute disparity, the second was a disparity grating containing both absolute and relative disparity. Responses both during the adaptation condition and after the adaptation period declined for the odd harmonics/grating stimulus but not the even harmonics/plane stimulus. Following adaptation, results also indicate higher neural thresholds for the disparity grating in the odd harmonics. Taken together, the data provides evidence that odd and even harmonics reflect the response of distinct disparity sub-systems. Additionally, as the first harmonic mirrored results recorded perceptually in psychophysics studies, there is further evidence that the first harmonic contains perceptually relevant information while the second contains perceptually irrelevant information. This is significant as SSVEPs examining the second harmonic could access biological systems under the psychophysical radar. Finally, the results suggest that the initial cortical stage of processing of absolute disparity does not adapt but the second stage of relative disparity processing does adapt.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
